3 TOP RECURRING SECOND-ORDER MECHANISMS

The new case strengthens the identity-to-authority-binding cluster. A
token obtained from a lower-trust surface can become a bridge when the
same bearer/session semantics are accepted by higher-trust systems.
This is structurally adjacent to inference-router identity
substitution, shared-cache credential crossover, plugin pin/resolution
confusion, and delegated-module authority composition.

4 STAGE/EDGE GRAPH

community app -> [CONFIRMED] auth token -> [CONFIRMED] cross-service
acceptance -> [CONFIRMED] internal GitHub/repository authority ->
[CONFIRMED] authenticated PR -> [UNKNOWN] CI/release/signer ->
[UNKNOWN] production action.

5 UNKNOWN LINKS WORTH RESOLVING

- Exact token class and why it was accepted across Discourse, ChatGPT
and GitHub.

- Whether GitHub authority could trigger CI or modify
protected/release-relevant branches.

- Whether token revocation alone removed all sessions/delegations
created with it.

- Whether the cross-service trust relationship was architectural SSO,
token exchange, or another mechanism.

6 DEFENSIVE CHOKEPOINTS + DETECTION PRIMITIVES

Bind tokens to explicit audience/service and purpose; prevent
community-facing session material from being replayable against
code/production planes; use short-lived scoped repository credentials;
require independent elevation for internal source and release
authority; correlate token issuance/use across trust domains; alert
when a credential first observed on a low-trust/community surface is
presented to GitHub, CI, cloud, signer or production systems; rotate
credentials and audit durable delegations/sessions created during
exposure.

QP implication: ALLOW(token T, service A) must not imply ALLOW(T,
service B). Authority proof should bind subject + audience +
capability + resource + expiry, and higher-trust transitions should
require a fresh proof rather than bearer-token portability.

7 SAFE LAB REPRODUCTION QUEUE

Build two disposable services representing forum and repository
planes, issue fake audience-bound tokens, and verify that a token
minted for the forum is rejected by the repository. Add a deliberately
misconfigured test gateway that accepts both audiences and demonstrate
only a harmless marker-file PR in a disposable repository. No real
credentials or third-party targets.

